  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and satellite/radar imagery show surface \nhigh pressure centered over the Great Lakes region early this \nafternoon, maintaining quiet and dry weather. Observations across \nnorthern and central Wisconsin indicate partly to mostly sunny \nskies with scattered fair weather cumulus, light and variable \nwinds, and temperatures generally in the 70s to lower 80s. Looking\nupstream, satellite imagery reveals mid and high clouds streaming\nacross the Dakotas and Minnesota within a region of stronger warm\nair advection and mid-level shortwave energy. As the high \npressure shifts east toward the New England coast tonight, the \nupstream shortwave trough and associated warm front will track \ninto the Upper Great Lakes, bringing the next chance for \nprecipitation by Saturday.\n\nThe initial forecast focus is on a progressive shortwave trough \nand weak surface warm advection pushing into the region on \nSaturday. High pressure shifting east tonight will allow mid and \nhigh clouds to gradually thicken and lower from west to east \novernight. Patchy fog may develop late across far northeast \nWisconsin if skies remain clear long enough, but no widespread \ntravel impacts are expected. \n\nBy Saturday morning, increasing isentropic ascent will push a \nband of rain into north-central and central Wisconsin, spreading \ninto eastern Wisconsin by the late morning and afternoon. Forecast\nsoundings indicate a relatively saturated column, but most-\nunstable CAPE values generally under 100 J/kg will limit \nwidespread thunderstorm coverage. While deep- layer shear is \nadequate, the lack of robust instability keeps the severe weather \nthreat very low, though an isolated rumble of thunder cannot be \nruled out. In general, widespread rainfall amounts of 0.20 to 0.40\ninches are anticipated. There is a 30-50% chance of rainfall \ngreater than 0.50 inches over central and east-central WI (lower \nprobabilities further north). The widespread precipitation and \ncloud cover will hold daytime high temperatures in the lower to \nmiddle 70s. Most of the steady rain is expected to exit to the \neast by late Saturday night.\n\nHeading into the latter half of the weekend and early next week, \nthe synoptic pattern transitions as upper-level ridging builds \nacross the eastern CONUS and the surface warm front slowly lifts \nnorth of the area. This setup will place northeast Wisconsin \nfirmly in the warm sector by Monday, with a considerably warmer \nand more humid airmass in place. High temperatures Sunday through\nTuesday will return into the 80s, with surface dewpoints rising \ninto the upper 60s to lower 70s. \n\nThe increasing heat and moisture will result in a notable jump in\ninstability, with MLCAPE values forecast to build between 2000 \nand 3000 J/kg during peak afternoon heating on Monday. Despite the\nrobust instability, precipitation chances appear relatively \nlimited Sunday and Monday as significant mid- level capping and \nweak large-scale forcing will largely suppress widespread \nconvective initiation. However, if the capping inversion can be \nbreached by subtle shortwave energy riding over the ridge, \nisolated to scattered strong thunderstorms would be possible given\nthe thermodynamic environment. Trends in thunderstorm potential\nwill need to be monitored in the coming shifts.  A slowly \napproaching cold front from the northwest will bring slightly \nhigher probabilities for showers and thunderstorms toward the \nTuesday and Wednesday timeframe, before a drier airmass and more \nseasonal temperatures return to the region later in the week.",
